A||N||11||sjp777||Anyone know anything about powder coating||||7||
Z||000000||sjp777||04-01-2001||11:18 PM||||I just got new rims for my 88GT. Problem is now you can really see the calipers. I want to powder coat them red, but the 88 calipers are aluminum. Can aluminum be powder coated, or does the lack of electrical conductivity prevent this?<P>Check out my car here: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A> ||||reg||7
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-01-2001||11:39 PM||||If you have original alloy calipers, clean them with aluminum cleaner to make them look good...<BR>If you powder coat them, they need to be stripped and overhauled after baking...<BR>If any caliper needs replacing, it will need to be powder coated, adding to the expense...<BR>Else, use engine enamal (high temp) to paint them...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||04-01-2001||11:47 PM||||I was planning on unbolting the outer half of the caliper and just powder coating that. That way the half with the piston wouldn't need to go in the oven. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||AkursedX||04-01-2001||11:50 PM||||I just used used high-temp engine paint on my calipers.  I sandblasted the outside part of it 1st though.  Then painted them silver.  I did that last May and they still are nice and shiney.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-02-2001||02:10 AM||||When I was having some of my parts powdercoated red (Plenum and valve covers)I was warned that some of the parts that get the hottest might fade to orange. I know that powdercoated exhaust (the guy had more money than he knew what to do with) pipes have a tendency to do that.<P>I know that Keith Huff had his original calipers powder coated orange and they held up just great.<P>I just started using Simple Green Automotive Degreaser (I dont' know how much different it is from the regular stuff but I found it in Pep Boys) and with a regular brush the calipers come out surpisingly clean and silver!!!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 04-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||04-02-2001||09:39 AM||||The plenum and valve covers are also aluminum, so I don't think there is a conductivity issue.  But as the calipers are behind the wheel, I think just painting with the high temp paint would yield similar results with a lot less $, but it's your car & $, so go for it, it'll work. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||04-02-2001||11:54 AM||||I just painted mines red with a new caliper paint being sold at PepBoys. Results was great. If you want pictures email me.||||reg||1
Z||000007||operator64||04-02-2001||12:04 PM||||   Aluminum is the third on the list for being the best electrical conductor. <BR>  No. 1 is Gold<BR>  No. 2 is Copper<BR>  No. 3 is Aluminum<BR>   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||04-02-2001||12:16 PM||||powder is applied with static electricity, so it dont need to conduct current anyway. I just use Hi-temp paint for calipers and hat myself, works fine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||04-02-2001||02:25 PM||||I was hoping that was a typo about aluminum not being a good conductor. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000010||theogre||04-02-2001||07:14 PM||||Try a brass brissle wire brush on them. won't dig into the aluminum like steel brissle. A wheel brush on a drill or grinder will make short work of them. (Don't put brass brush on a regular grinder. has to be a belt driven low speed job. Brass won't take the RPM of a direct drive grinder.)||||reg||1
Z||000011||JSocha||04-02-2001||11:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sjp777:<BR><B>I just got new rims for my 88GT. Problem is now you can really see the calipers. I want to powder coat them red, but the 88 calipers are aluminum. Can aluminum be powder coated, or does the lack of electrical conductivity prevent this?<P>Check out my car here: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There is "low" temperature powder available that will not hurt sensitive/criticle parts.  We use it on our hydraulic cylinders at work which go through a bake oven for about 60 minutes and it does not effect the rubber o-rings and/or nylon wiper seals.  Email me for more information if you are still interested as I can find out more from our engineer at work.<P><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||3
